// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`what the bot knows, for review > matches the English snapshot: any change to it shows here 1`] = `
"[docs] Docs · How tonkfun.fun works.
Plain answers before you put money in: how a launch works, every fee, the locks and the launch-block tax, what graduation means, and the risks.

[docs.about] What tonkfun.fun is
A token terminal for Solana and Robinhood Chain, in five screens:
- Explore: Every new token across both chains, from the major launchpads, each with its Mission score.
- Swap: Trade tokens by the best route found, signed in your own wallet.
- Bridge: Move major assets between 17 networks, into Robinhood Chain above all, by the best live route.
- Portfolio: Your wallets' holdings and activity, read live from the chains. It only reads: it never signs or sends.
- Launch: The Creator Studio, where you launch a token on tonkfun.fun's own launchpad on Robinhood Chain.

[docs.launching] Launching a token
Tokens launch from the Creator Studio, on Launch, in four steps:
- Token & basics: The name, ticker, description and links. The name and ticker are permanent; the description and links can be edited later.
- Locked Mission: Whether to lock part of your dev buy, how much and for how long.
- Mission economics: Your Crew Rewards and Creator Rewards, on top of the 1% protocol fee.
- Token Agent: An optional persona and mission brief for your token, stored on-chain with it. Its chat is a preview for now.
The dev buy:
You can buy your own token inside the launch transaction. That dev buy is exempt from the launch-block tax. It pays the 1% protocol fee and your own Creator fee, which comes straight back to you, but no Crew fee, since nobody else holds the token yet. You need a dev buy to lock tokens, and a lock to set Creator Rewards above 0.5%.
The review:
Before anything is sent, a final review shows everything you're about to launch. You pay the launch fee and the dev buy in one transaction, signed in your own wallet, and the launch counts once its receipt is confirmed.
A token can have an image: a PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF up to 2 MB, added at launch or later in Edit details. Without one, it shows its first letter.

[docs.curve] The bonding curve
Every tonkfun.fun token starts on its own bonding curve. The launchpad is the market: you buy from the curve and sell back to it, and the curve sets the price. Each buy raises it, and each sale lowers it.
The price comes from virtual reserves of ETH and tokens, whose product stays the same through every trade. They're virtual because the curve starts with no real ETH: the starting reserve only sets the starting price.
Every token has a fixed supply of 1 billion, all minted to the launchpad at launch: 800 million are sold on the curve, and 200 million are kept back for its pool. Nobody can mint more or freeze transfers. A sale pays out at most the ETH really in the curve.
Live figures, read with launchpad_figures: Starting reserves; Starting market cap; Graduation.
Read live from the launchpad on Robinhood Chain.
These settings apply to new launches. Each token keeps the reserves it launched with.
Graduation:
When the curve has sold all 800 million tokens, the token graduates in that same transaction. The buy that completes the curve is cut to exactly what it needs, and the rest of its ETH is refunded.
The curve's ETH, less 1.5% (1% to tonkfun.fun and a 0.5% bonus to the creator), goes with the 200 million tokens into a new Uniswap v4 pool, ETH against the token. Curve trading ends there; from then on the token trades in its pool.
The pool's liquidity is locked for good: the migrator holds the position and can only collect its fees, never withdraw it.
Only the migrator can open that pool. A guard on it turns anyone else away, so nobody can open it early at a rigged price. Trading in it is standard Uniswap v4.

[docs.fees] Fees
Launching costs a one-time launch fee, paid to tonkfun.fun's treasury.
Live figures, read with launchpad_figures: Launch fee.
Read live from the launchpad on Robinhood Chain.
On every curve trade:
Every buy and sale on the curve pays its fee in ETH: a fixed 1% protocol fee to tonkfun.fun, plus the token's Crew Rewards for its holders and Creator Rewards for its creator.
Crew and Creator Rewards share at most 4%, so the fees on a curve trade never come to more than 5% in all. Crew Rewards are either off or 1% or more. The creator sets both at launch, and they never change.
Creator Rewards tiers:
- Up to 0.5%: no lock needed.
- Up to 2%, Standard: 50% or more of the dev buy locked for 30 days or more.
- Up to 4%, Ironclad: 80% or more of the dev buy locked for 90 days or more.
Above 0.5%, the lock must also hold at least 10 million tokens: 1% of supply.
The Crew fee is only charged once eligible holders own at least 10 million tokens, as Crew Rewards explains below.
Referrals:
A curve trade made through someone's referral link pays them 25% of the protocol fee: 0.25% of the trade, out of tonkfun.fun's 1%, not on top of it. Your own link earns nothing on your own trades, and the launch fee and the creator's dev buy carry no referral share.
At graduation:
1.5% of the curve's ETH: 1% to tonkfun.fun and 0.5% to the creator as a graduation bonus.
After graduation:
The pool's fee is the token's full fee: 1% plus its Crew and Creator Rewards. A token with 2% Crew Rewards and 1% Creator Rewards has a 4% pool fee.
The pool's fees build up until someone shares them with Share pool fees on the token page. Anyone can, for just the network fee. The ETH side is split in the same proportions as the fee, between tonkfun.fun, the Crew and the creator, and the token side is sent to the dead address, out of circulation.

[docs.locks] Locks
A creator can lock part of their dev buy, written into the launch itself. The launchpad holds the locked tokens, and nobody can take them early, the owner included.
- Time-lock: Everything unlocks at the end.
- Linear vesting: Tokens unlock continuously, second by second, until the end.
- No lock: The creator's tokens are theirs at once.
A lock needs a dev buy, covers 50% to 100% of it, and runs for 7 days or more.
Unlocked tokens stay in the launchpad until the creator claims them on the token page.
Extending a lock:
A lock can be made longer, by 7 days or more at a time, but never shorter. The new end counts from the old one, or from now if the old end has passed.
Extending a linear vest restarts it: what has already unlocked stays claimable, and only what hasn't vests again, from now to the new end.
The Locked badge:
- Locked: A tonkfun.fun launch wears the Locked badge while its lock will still hold at least 10 million tokens (1% of supply) a week from now. Locks on other launchpads count in the Mission score but don't earn the badge.

[docs.crew] Crew Rewards
When the creator turns them on, Crew Rewards share 1% to 4% of every curve trade with the token's holders: in ETH, and in proportion to what each holds.
Every holder is eligible except the launchpad itself (which holds the unsold curve tokens and the creator's locked ones), the token's own contract, its Uniswap pool, the burn addresses, and any contract the owner excludes, such as a vault. Only contracts can be excluded, and only for good.
The 10 million minimum:
No Crew fee is charged until eligible holders own at least 10 million tokens: 1% of supply. Until then, curve trades pay only the protocol fee and Creator Rewards, so a tiny holding can never collect the whole Crew fee.
Getting paid:
Rewards build up as trades happen. They're sent to your wallet automatically whenever your balance of the token changes, and anything unpaid can be claimed on the token page at any time.
After graduation:
The Crew's share of the pool fees reaches holders the same way, each time someone shares the fees. If eligible holders own less than 10 million tokens at that moment, it goes to tonkfun.fun's treasury instead, so later holders never inherit it.

[docs.tax] The launch-block tax
- Block-0 Shield: In the launch block and the next one, every buy pays a 99% tax, the creator's own later buys included. Only the dev buy made inside the launch transaction is exempt.
The tax is burned: sent to the dead address, where nobody can ever spend it, tonkfun.fun included. The other 1% of the buy goes through the curve as usual.
It keeps bots from sniping a token the moment it launches. The blocks are Robinhood Chain's own, counted by the chain, not by a timer.
To spare you the tax, this site sends no buys until two blocks after launch. If a buy doesn't go through in the first seconds, wait a moment and try again.

[docs.score] Mission score and badges
Every token on Explore has a Mission score from 1 to 99, worked out from what the chain and the market show. It errs on the side of caution. It's a guide, not advice: no score makes a token safe to buy.
- Safe: 75 and up: the checks turned up little to worry about.
- Caution: 50 to 74: something calls for a closer look, such as a token under ten minutes old or a creator buy that isn't locked.
- Risky: Under 50: clear warning signs, such as a creator who can still mint or freeze the token, a few wallets holding most of the supply, or a pool too thin for its price.
What it weighs:
It starts at 50, then adds points for good signs and takes them away for warnings: whether anyone can still mint or freeze the token, whether its pool's liquidity is locked or burned, the creator's buy and how much of it is locked, Crew Rewards of 1% or more, how concentrated the holders are, the last hour's trading and price, the day's volume, the pool's depth, and the first ten minutes, which are too early to tell.
A tonkfun.fun launch is scored from its own chain facts: a fixed supply nobody can freeze, the creator's dev buy and lock, its Crew Rewards and its age.
Badges:
- Locked: A tonkfun.fun launch whose lock will still hold 1% of supply or more a week from now.
- Crew 2%: Crew Rewards of 1% or more on every curve trade; the badge shows the share.
- Agent: The creator set up a Token Agent: a persona and mission brief stored on-chain with the token.
- Hot: 20 or more buys in the last 15 minutes.
- New: Launched less than 30 minutes ago.
- Graduated: Its bonding curve has sold out, and it now trades in a pool.
- Verified RWA: A tokenized stock or real-world asset from a verified issuer, named in the badge's tooltip.
- Block-0 Shield: A tonkfun.fun launch, with the 99% launch-block tax described above.

[docs.safety] Safety
What was checked, and how to report a flaw: the Security page
Starting limits:
The owner sets the starting ETH reserve for new launches, between 1 and 100 ETH. A curve never holds more ETH than it takes to graduate, so that reserve also limits how much ETH any one curve can hold.
Live figures, read with launchpad_figures: Starting ETH reserve; Most ETH a new curve holds.
Who controls what:
The contracts are owned by the team's Safe account. The owner can pause and unpause the launchpad, set the launch fee (up to 0.05 ETH) and the starting ETH reserve, choose the treasury that receives fees, and exclude a contract from Crew Rewards.
The owner can't take the ETH in a curve, a creator's locked tokens or a pool's liquidity. It can't change a token's fees or lock, or the migrator, which was set once for good, and it can't give up ownership.
If the launchpad is paused:
A pause stops new launches and buys on the curve. Selling to the curve always stays open: the contract's sell can't be paused. Claims, withdrawals, lock extensions and edits keep working, and graduated tokens keep trading in their pools.
Live figures, read with launchpad_figures: The launchpad now.
Read live from the launchpad on Robinhood Chain.
The contracts:
On Robinhood Chain mainnet: each contract, its source verified on Blockscout, then the Safe account that owns them.

[docs.faq] FAQ
Q: Why didn't my buy go through in the first seconds?
A: In the launch block and the next one, every buy except the creator's dev buy loses 99% to the launch-block tax, so this site sends no buys until two blocks after launch. Wait a moment and try again.
Q: When does my token graduate?
A: The moment its curve has sold all 800 million tokens. The bonding curve section shows how much ETH that takes for a new launch today.
Q: Where do the fees go?
A: The launch fee and the 1% protocol fee go to tonkfun.fun's treasury, less a referrer's 25% share of the protocol fee. Crew Rewards go to the token's eligible holders and Creator Rewards to its creator. At graduation, 1% of the curve's ETH goes to the treasury and 0.5% to the creator.
Q: Can the creator sell their locked tokens?
A: Not before they unlock. The launchpad holds them, the creator can claim only what has unlocked, and a lock can be extended but never shortened.
Q: Can I always sell?
A: To the curve, yes, even while the launchpad is paused: a sale pays out at most the ETH really in the curve. After graduation, you sell in the token's pool.
Q: Why is there no Crew fee on my trade?
A: The Crew fee starts once eligible holders own at least 10 million tokens, 1% of supply. Until then, trades pay only the protocol fee and Creator Rewards.
Q: How do I get my Crew Rewards?
A: They're sent to your wallet whenever your balance of the token changes. Anything unpaid can be claimed on the token page at any time.
Q: Is the liquidity locked after graduation?
A: Yes, for good. The migrator holds the pool position and can only collect its fees: nobody can withdraw the liquidity, tonkfun.fun included.
Q: Can a token's fees or lock change after launch?
A: The fees never change, and a lock can only be made longer. The creator can edit the description, links and agent brief; the name and ticker stay as they are.
Q: Has the launchpad been audited?
A: Not by an outside auditor: it has had internal reviews only. The Security page lists what was checked.

[security] Security · Found a flaw? Tell us first.
How to report a vulnerability in tonkfun.fun's launchpad contracts, what's in scope, the rules, and the rewards.
Report a vulnerability:
Report it privately, and to us first: not in public, and not to anyone else until it's fixed.
What to include:
- The contract and function, with its address if it's deployed.
- What an attacker could do, and who would lose what.
- Steps to reproduce it. A Foundry test on a fork is ideal.
- How to reach you, and where to send a reward.
What to expect:
We reply within 48 hours, keep you posted while we fix it, and tell you when it's done.
Scope:
The launchpad's contracts on Robinhood Chain mainnet, exactly as frozen for release. Each file's SHA-256 lets you check that you're reading the same source.
- Launchpad
- Graduation migrator
- Pool guard
Out of scope:
- The website's own hosting.
- Third-party services, such as Uniswap, wallets and bridges. Report those to their own teams.
- Social engineering, phishing included, of our team or our users.
Rules:
- Never test against mainnet funds that aren't yours. Use a fork, the testnet or your own wallets.
- No denial of service, and nothing that slows the site or the chain down for others.
- Give us time to fix it before any disclosure. We'll agree the date with you.
Safe harbour:
We consider good-faith research that follows these rules authorised. We won't take or support legal action against you for it, and if anyone else does, we'll make it known that you followed this policy.
Rewards:
A valid report earns a reward by its severity.
- Critical: Theft or permanent freezing of funds, or taking control of the contracts.
- High: Theft of fees or rewards, or funds frozen for a time.
- Medium: A contract breaks one of its own rules, with no funds lost.
- Low: A contract doesn't do what it says, with no funds at risk.
What was checked:
Before release, the contracts went through:
- An internal review and two re-reviews of its fixes, plus an independent review, all by AI models.
- Static analysis with Slither and Aderyn, with every finding triaged.
- Invariant fuzzing with Foundry and Medusa, with no violation found.
- Proofs of the curve and fee maths: symbolic with Halmos, and by hand where the solver timed out.
- A test for each known launchpad attack, such as a rigged pool price or a sandwiched graduation.
- A full rehearsal against a copy of mainnet: launch, trade, graduate and pause.
- Campaigns on testnet with real wallets.

[privacy] Privacy · The bot keeps very little, and not for long.
How TonkFun, tonkfun.fun's bot on Telegram (@TonkfunBot), handles what you send it.
What it reads:
Only what it needs to answer you and to keep tonkfun.fun's official group safe:
- In a private chat, the messages you send it: a question, a token address, a wallet address for a referral link or a command.
- In tonkfun.fun's official community group, where it is an admin, Telegram shows it every message, edit and new member. It reads them to answer questions, remove spam and scams, check that new members are human and tell the team when someone needs a person.
- In any other group, only its commands, such as /check (with the message a /check replies to), and replies to it. Telegram also shows it notices such as who joined, which it ignores. If such a group makes it an admin, Telegram shows it every message there; it still acts only on its commands and keeps nothing.
- When you type @TonkfunBot in any chat, the text you type after it, as you type it.
- The buttons you tap on its messages.
With each of these, Telegram includes your Telegram ID, name and app language. The bot uses your ID to pace its replies and, in the official group, for the records below; your name only to spot spam and accounts posing as the team, and in its notes to the team; your language to answer in yours.
AI answers and checks:
Its answers, and its checks of messages its own rules can't settle, are written by Claude, an AI model made by Anthropic, which processes them for tonkfun.fun as a service provider.
It sends Anthropic text, never photos or files: a question you ask it (with /ask, by mentioning it, by replying to it, or with the Ask button in a private chat); its own earlier answer when you reply to one; and your message when someone uses /ask in reply to it. In the official group it also sends messages its rules can't settle on their own: questions; messages with handles, wallet or token addresses, or links that aren't on its allow-list (whether it removed them or not); messages that ask for help or mention a problem, such as lost funds or a failed buy; messages that warn about, ask about or report a scam, or mention DMs; messages that may show someone is in distress; and new members' messages of 3 words or more. With them it sends a few facts its code works out, such as your app's language and, for its checks, whether you're a new or trusted member, whether the message was edited or forwarded, and its links' domains. It adds no Telegram ID, name or username and hides e-mail addresses and phone numbers in the formats it recognises, but otherwise sends the message as it was written, so keep personal details out of it.
With each request it also sends a code made from your Telegram ID, which changes every month and which Anthropic can't turn back into your ID, so Anthropic can spot misuse.
Anthropic deletes this text within 30 days. If its safety systems flag a message, it may keep it for up to 2 years, and its safety scores for up to 7 years; data it has made anonymous may be kept longer. By default Anthropic doesn't use it to train its models, and tonkfun.fun hasn't opted in.
Never send a seed phrase or a private key. The bot removes anything that looks like one, in the group and in what you ask it, and never sends one anywhere.
AI answers can be wrong and are never financial advice. Each one says so.
Some moderation is automatic: its rules, and in some cases Claude's check, can remove a message, mute someone or remove them from the group. Every action is reported to the team, who can undo it, and a person decides every ban the rules aren't certain of. If it mutes or bans you, or records a warning, tap ‘Why? Ask for a review’ on its notice.
What it keeps:
No chat history, and nothing from private chats once it has answered.
- While it works, it holds some things in memory only, then forgets them: user IDs, names, usernames and the text of a message for at most 10 minutes (to pace replies, stop floods and raids, check a message, see whether someone else answers a question first, gather its notes to the team, or wait for you to tap Ask or Share); the group's list of admins, read from Telegram every 10 minutes; and, without user IDs, which of its answers replies to which message, for 2 days.
- A wallet address you send for a referral link is used to make the link, then dropped.
In the official group only, it keeps these records on its server, then deletes them:
- Warnings: your Telegram ID, how many you have and the reason for the last one, until 7 days after the last.
- New members: your ID and when you joined or passed the check (or first commented on a channel post without joining), for your first days (1 to 7, by the group's setting), while stricter rules apply.
- A check in progress: your ID and the check message, for at most 15 minutes (45 if Telegram delays posting the check).
- Trusted members: your ID and a fingerprint of your name, if an admin marks you trusted, until an admin removes it or you leave the group.
- Reviews: when you last asked for one, for a day.
- Messages still to delete, its own and members' messages it removed: their IDs, for at most 2 days.
When you leave the group, it deletes your records there. A removal stays with Telegram.
When it removes a message or restricts someone (or, while the group is in observe mode, would have), when a case needs a person, when you report a message or ask for a review, or when it proposes an answer in observe mode, it posts in the team's private admin chat on Telegram: your name, username and ID, a short excerpt, a link to the message and, where Claude checked the message, the AI's guess; if you're a new member, also how long ago you joined or passed the check. A report also shows who sent it (their name, username and ID) and their reason, and a review your current restriction. It also posts there your name, username and ID with an excerpt of your question when Claude refuses two of your questions in the group within 10 minutes; your name and username when an admin marks you trusted or stops trusting you; and each trusted member's name, username and ID when an admin asks for the list. It deletes its routine notes there after 2 days, and the chat deletes everything after 7; the bot keeps no copy. From a private chat it posts your name, username and message only if you tap Share with the team or use /report.
It also counts each day's answers, reports, checks and moderation actions, and its AI spending, with no IDs.
What it never does:
- Ask for your seed phrase, private keys or passwords.
- Sign, send or approve a transaction. It has no wallet and holds no wallet keys.
- Message you first in a private chat. The team never DMs first either: anyone who does is not us.
Anything that trades, launches or claims happens on tonkfun.fun, which its buttons open in your browser. There you review it and sign in your own wallet.
Your choices and rights:
The bot is run by the tonkfun.fun team for tonkfun.fun's community.
It uses what it reads only to answer questions and keep the group free of spam and scams.
Only Anthropic (for AI answers and checks, in the United States, under its data processing terms with the EU's standard contractual clauses), Telegram (which carries every message) and Railway (which hosts the bot's server and stores its records and logs) receive it.
In a private chat, nothing goes to the AI unless you use /ask or tap Ask. In the group, moderation applies to everyone, and while unprompted answers are on, the bot may also answer a question about tonkfun.fun that you post without asking it.
You can ask for a copy of your records, or for them to be deleted sooner, through the contact on the Security page, and you can complain to your data protection authority.
The official channel:
Posts in tonkfun.fun's official Telegram channel use only public data from the launchpad on Robinhood Chain, and never name a wallet.
Telegram:
Telegram itself carries your messages, under its own rules. Chats with bots are cloud chats, not end-to-end encrypted.
Questions:
To ask about this policy, or to have your records deleted sooner, use the contact on the Security page.

[features.wallet] Wallet
- Connecting shares only your address. Nothing is signed.
- MetaMask may say this is likely to fail. Its preview can't read Robinhood Chain's block number, which the Block-0 Shield uses. Before your wallet opens, this site checks this exact transaction against the chain.
- Not seen on the chain yet: The chain still hasn't seen this transaction, so your wallet most likely didn't send it. If your wallet shows it as sent, queued or pending, don't send it again. This keeps checking.
- Launch not seen on the chain yet: The chain still hasn't seen this launch, so your wallet most likely didn't send it. If your wallet shows it as sent, queued or pending, don't launch again. This page keeps checking.
- Still confirming: This is taking longer than usual. Check your wallet's activity before trying again; this keeps checking.
- This is taking longer than usual. Check your wallet's activity before launching again; this page keeps checking.
- This transaction was replaced or cancelled in your wallet. Nothing was launched by it.
- It failed on-chain. Nothing was launched; only the network fee was spent.
- It failed on-chain. Only the network fee was spent.

[features.launch] Launch (Creator Studio)
- Initial dev buy: Exempt from the launch-block tax. Trade fees are included in your buy.
- An initial dev buy is required to lock tokens and unlock higher Creator Rewards.
- Socials & links: Stored with your mission and shown on Explore and the token page. You can update them later.
- Token name: Enter a token name with 1–32 characters.
- Ticker: Use 1–10 letters or numbers, without a dollar sign.
- Description: Keep the description within 280 characters.
- X handle: Enter an X handle or a valid X profile link.
- Telegram: Enter a valid t.me link, or leave this empty.
- Website: Enter a website domain or an http/https URL.
- Lock duration: Enter 7 to 365 days.
- Vesting period: Each month is 30 days.
- Agent name: Enter an agent name with 1–32 characters.
- Role: Enter a role with 1–50 characters.
- Personality & mission brief: Enter an agent brief with 1–400 characters.
- Ironclad commitment: 80%+ for 90+ days. Creator Rewards can reach 4.0% within the shared reward pool.
- Standard commitment: 50%+ for 30+ days. Creator Rewards can reach 2.0%. Lock 80%+ for 90+ days to reach 4.0%.
- No qualifying lock: Creator Rewards are capped at 0.5%. Lock 50%+ for 30+ days to unlock more.
- Mission score: +10 for this lock.
- Mission score: no lock points. Keep over half your buy, and at least 10,000,000 tokens, locked past day 30 to earn +10.
- No Locked badge: the lock must still hold 1% of supply a week after launch.
- Crew Rewards: Minimum 1% for the Crew badge. The curve, locked dev tokens and excluded system addresses do not earn rewards.
- Crew and Creator share a 4% pool. Changing one can reduce the other.
- Standard and Ironclad also need 10,000,000 tokens locked: 1% of supply.
- With no initial buy, the dev lock is skipped and Creator Rewards are capped at 0.5%.
- What's included? The dev buy includes regular trading fees, and your Creator fee on it returns to you in the same transaction. The 0.5% creator graduation bonus is paid once from the curve's ETH when it completes. Fees and the lock are set at launch; metadata and agent details can be updated, and a lock can be extended.
- One final look. Trading fees are included in the dev buy. Fees and the lock are committed at launch. Metadata can change and locks can be extended.
- Token Agent: This chat is a preview: no agent service answers yet.
- Mission score preview: Trading and holders move the score after launch.
- Launches are stopped while we check the launchpad's settings. Nothing was sent.
- Launch confirming: Keep this page open, or come back to this link. Nothing is sent twice.

[features.images] Token image
- PNG, JPEG, WebP or GIF, up to 2 MB. Cropped to a square; an animated GIF keeps its first frame.
- Confirm in your wallet to upload. Signing is free and sends nothing.
- You've uploaded 10 images in the last hour. Try again later.

[features.token] Token Detail
- This token launched seconds ago. Buys open two blocks after launch.
- The curve holds less than this sale is worth, so it pays out what it has.
- Graduated: trading moves to its Uniswap pool
- This token has graduated, so curve trading is over. Nothing was sent.
- Buying is stopped while we check the launchpad's settings. Selling still works. Nothing was sent.
- Your approval went through, but the sale wasn't sent. Review the sale again to send it.
- The launchpad is paused: buying on the curve is on hold for now. Selling stays open, and claims, withdrawals, lock extensions and edits still work.
- A time-lock unlocks all at once at its end.
- Extend lock: A lock can be made longer, never shorter. Your vest restarts: what has unlocked stays claimable, and the rest vests again from now to the new end.
- Days to add: Add between 7 and 3,650 days.
- Edit details: Name and ticker can't change. What you save here replaces the details and the agent brief on-chain.
- Only the token's creator can do this.

[features.swap] Swap
- Your wallet signs every swap. Nothing moves without your approval.
- Slippage: If the price moves more than this before your swap lands, it won't go through.
- Choose a token: No match. Paste the token's address to find it.
- Recent: Swaps you make here are listed on this device.
- The price moved before it landed, or funds were short. Check the new quote and try again.
- It failed on-chain. Nothing was exchanged.

[features.bridge] Bridge
- 1. Pick the networks. Choose where your assets start and where you want them to arrive.
- 2. Check the details. Review the asset, amount, destination address, fees and estimated time.
- 3. Follow the journey. A transfer status keeps you informed through to arrival.
- Which routes? We compare Relay, Across, LI.FI and, for ETH from Ethereum to Robinhood Chain, the canonical bridge. Going back through the canonical bridge takes about 7 days, so leaving uses a fast route.
- Your wallet signs every transfer. Nothing moves without your approval.
- Check your destination. Double-check it: a transfer can't be undone once it has left.
- Taking longer than usual. It's still being followed: you can close this and check back in Recent.
- If funds left your wallet, keep the transaction link: the route can trace it.
- Your transfers: Saved on this device only.

[features.portfolio] Portfolio
- Your balances, positions and activity across Robinhood Chain and Solana, in one place. Connecting only shares your address; nothing is signed.
- These have no reliable price, so they count for nothing in your total. Unknown tokens are often spam: don't follow links or instructions in their names.
- Live balances. Prices from Jupiter and DexScreener.
- Read from the chains. Spam airdrops are left out.
- Your balances couldn't be read. The networks didn't answer. Your funds aren't affected.
- Send only Robinhood Chain assets to this address. Assets sent from another network can be lost.
- Send only Solana assets to this address. Assets sent from another network can be lost.

[features.alerts] Alerts
- Checked every 30 seconds while tonkfun.fun is open in a tab. Kept in this browser only.
- Launchpads: Every new launch; Graduations; Hot launches
- Hot: launched within the hour, with 20 or more buys in 15 minutes.
- Tokens I watch: A token I watch graduates; A token I watch has its creator lock end, claimed or extended
- Watch a token with the star on its page.
- No alerts yet. Set one with the bell on any token page.
- Browser notifications: Shows alerts outside the page while a tonkfun.fun tab is open.
- You have 25 alerts, the most there can be. Delete one first.
- Alerts aren't on testnet yet.

